(B) THE DEGENERACY DECOMPOSITION -- what quantity is actually being predicted?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The reionization efficiency in the model that produced the number above is
      zeta = A_He * f_star * f_esc * N_ion ,
  where f_star is the fraction of a halo's baryons turned into stars, f_esc the escape fraction, and N_ion
  the ionizing photons produced per stellar baryon (the quantity JWST reports as xi_ion, per unit UV
  luminosity rather than per baryon, but linearly related to it at fixed spectral shape).
  EVERY FACTOR ENTERS LINEARLY.  So the framework's requirement is a statement about the PRODUCT, and any
  one factor can absorb the whole of it.  Written out for the branch the candidate quotes:

           if the shift is carried entirely by    required change
                                   f_esc alone   x 0.599 to 0.707
                                  xi_ion alone   x 0.599 to 0.707
                                  f_star alone   x 0.599 to 0.707
           f_esc x xi_ion (what JWST measures)   x 0.599 to 0.707

  There is nothing in the framework that says WHICH factor moves.  f_star at z = 6-9 is not measured
  independently of the same halo-abundance modelling this calculation is doing; it is inferred from the
  luminosity function, which the framework ALSO changes (section D).  So the candidate's headline claim --
  'the cosmological constant predicts the escape fraction' -- is a prediction of f_star*f_esc*xi_ion, one of
  whose three factors is unmeasured and is set by the same data the framework re-interprets.
  [FAIL] B1 CRITERION (1): a Kepler-grade law relates MEASURED quantities.  This one constrains a triple product of which JWST measures two factors and infers the third from the same abundance modelling.  This check passes only if the prediction can be assigned to a directly measured quantity, and it cannot   (zeta = A_He f_star f_esc N_ion; the framework fixes only the product, to x0.60-0.71.  f_star is degenerate with the whole of it at exponent 1.)

(C) THE EFFICIENCY-FREE PART -- renormalise zeta to hit Planck exactly, then look at the SHAPE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A normalisation cannot change the shape of the history.  If, once each branch is put back on Planck's
  tau_e, the ionized-fraction history is indistinguishable from LambdaCDM's, then the candidate carries no
  information that survives the efficiency degeneracy, and it dies here.  If it IS different, that difference
  is a real efficiency-free prediction, confrontable with the Lyman-alpha forest.

