(E) THE RESTATEMENT TEST -- executed
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Try to derive the relation from v^4 = G M_b a_0 plus algebra.  The deep-MOND limit is a statement about a
  CIRCULAR ORBIT in a static potential: it fixes v given M_b and a_0.  The chain here is
      a_0 -> nu(g_N/a_0) in the RADIAL equation of motion -> collapse time from turnaround -> (1+z_form) gain
      -> F_coll(z) -> dQ/dz -> tau_e.
  Nothing in v^4 = G M_b a_0 refers to a time, and no algebra on it produces one: the relation has no
  dimension of time in it at all once G, M_b and a_0 are given (v is a velocity; t would need a length).
  Explicitly: from v^4 = G M_b a_0 the only time that can be formed is v/a_0 = (G M_b/a_0)^{1/4}/a_0^{1/2},
  which is the deep-MOND crossing time of the SAME object, not the collapse time of a shell around it, and
  it carries no dependence on the turnaround radius, which is what the collapse integral is a functional of.
  numerically, for a 1e10 Msun halo at z = 10: the deep-MOND crossing time v/a_0 = 1.128e+15 s, the actual
  Route A collapse time from turnaround = 4.013e+15 s, a ratio of 3.56 -- and the ratio is a
  function of redshift through r_ta, which v^4 = G M_b a_0 knows nothing about.
  [PASS] E1 RESTATEMENT TEST: the relation does NOT close from v^4 = G M_b a_0 plus algebra.  IS_RESTATEMENT = FALSE.  This is a genuinely independent use of a_0 -- which is a point in the candidate's favour and is recorded as such, even though the candidate fails on other criteria   (collapse time / deep-MOND crossing time = 3.56 at z = 10 and the ratio is redshift-dependent)

(F) THE LEVERS, EACH MEASURED BY RE-RUNNING THE PIPELINE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE UPSILON LEVER IS EXACTLY ZERO: no stellar mass-to-light ratio appears anywhere in this chain.  The
  observable is per unit UV luminosity, the halo side is a mass function normalised by sigma_8, and the
  collapse side is a_0 and the background.  Verified by construction: grep the script for Upsilon and there
  is none, and re-running at 'Upsilon x 1.5' changes nothing because there is nothing to change.
  But the candidate is not therefore free of calibration.  Here are the levers that DO bite, each measured:

                               lever         x1       x1.5  d log(ratio)/d log(lever)
                             sigma_8     0.7074     0.8205                     0.3658
    T_vir (atomic-cooling threshold)     0.7074     0.7002                    -0.0250
             C_HII (clumping factor)     0.7074     0.7169                     0.0331
               Upsilon (stellar M/L)     0.7074     0.7074                     0.0000
  [PASS] F1 THE UPSILON LEVER IS ZERO, MEASURED not asserted: re-running the whole pipeline with 'Upsilon x1.5' returns the identical number, because no stellar mass-to-light ratio enters   (d log(zeta ratio)/d log Upsilon = 0.00e+00)
  [PASS] F2 but the prediction IS calibration-limited by the halo-model inputs, and the largest of those levers is quoted here.  This check passes only if every lever is small enough that a 50% error in it moves the prediction by less than the 25-59% shift the candidate is claiming to predict   (sigma_8: +0.366; T_vir (atomic-cooling threshold): -0.025; C_HII (clumping factor): +0.033; Upsilon (stellar M/L): +0.000.  A 50% error moves the ratio by 0.064 dex, 0.004 dex, 0.006 dex, 0.000 dex; the claimed signal is 0.150-0.223 dex.)

(G) MUTATION CONTROLS AND THE LambdaCDM ALTERNATIVE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  [PASS] G1 MUTATION: a collapse gain pinned to 1 (equivalently a_0 -> 0, the kernel the identity) must return the LambdaCDM efficiency exactly, so the whole effect is the a_0 term   (zeta(gain = 1)/zeta_LCDM = 1.00000000)
  [PASS] G2 MUTATION: with a_0 driven to zero the collapse time must equal Newton's exactly   (s(a_0 -> 0) = 1.000000)
  [FAIL] G3 THE LambdaCDM ALTERNATIVE, COMPUTED BESIDE: Planck's own 1-sigma range on tau_e already permits the LambdaCDM efficiency to move by the factor below.  This check passes only if the framework's demanded shift is LARGER than the shift Planck's error bar alone allows -- otherwise the 'prediction' is inside the existing uncertainty on the thing it is predicted from   (Planck's +-1 sigma on tau_e alone spans zeta = 9.93-16.97, a factor 1.71; the framework demands a factor 1.41-1.67)

========================================================================================================================
VERDICT
========================================================================================================================
  Read the checks.  What survives and what does not:
   SURVIVES: the restatement test (E1) -- this really is an independent use of a_0, not the RAR in disguise;
             and the Upsilon lever really is exactly zero (F1), which is more than most of the hunt can say.
   FAILS   : the claimed size (A1) -- the range is 0.60-0.71, not 0.41-0.75; the candidate's lower end is
             1/(F_coll boost), which is not the recalibrated efficiency, and overstates the effect 1.5x;
             criterion (1), because the predicted quantity is a triple product one of whose factors (f_star)
             is unmeasured and degenerate with the whole shift at exponent 1 (B1);
             criterion (3) on the shape, the only efficiency-free part, which is below measurement (C1b);
             and the independence test against item 73 (D1).  Criterion (2) survives only marginally: the
             coefficient moves 23% of itself between the two natural anchors, tau_e and the end of
             reionization (C3, a pass at the 25% threshold set before the number was known).
             And the shift it asks for is SMALLER than the range Planck's own error bar on tau_e already
             permits for the LambdaCDM efficiency (G3), so it is inside the existing uncertainty.
  ⚠ ONE OF MY OWN CLAIMS WITHDRAWN INSIDE THIS SCRIPT.  The first version found that the framework, put back
  on Planck's tau_e, never finishes reionizing (Q peaking at 0.66-0.81 and declining) -- which would have
  been a large framework kill.  It was a table-edge artefact in the shifted collapsed-fraction curve, is
  fixed here, and the claim is withdrawn.  With the fix the framework completes reionization in every branch
  and survives the forest-versus-CMB pincer at 1.5 sigma (C2).
  What is left on the liability side is small and real: at fixed tau_e the framework's reionization is more
  extended and leaves the gas up to 0.16 more neutral at z = 5.3 than LambdaCDM's, which is at the very edge
  of what the z = 5-6 neutral-fraction measurements can see.  That, not the ionizing budget, is where this
  chain could one day bite.

